94: A skill is a file on the Agent page, `AgentUI/src/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`: frontmatter with its `name` and a `trigger`, a `description` or both, then the text the model gets. The page sends every skill once per connection with the `skills` bridge message. A skill with a trigger is the app's: when the event it names happens, the host adds it to the one request where the model first reads about the event, as the request's last item, and to no later request. A skill with a description is the model's: `skill_read` lists the described skills in its own description and returns one by name, and is offered only while at least one exists (none does yet). The first skill is `file-loaded` (trigger `file_loaded`): the first message after a file is opened or imported is a judgement about the load report. It fires when the person opens or drops a file, after an in-app `object_import` (and a failed `project_open`) in the same turn, and in the opened project's chat after an in-app `project_open`, which replaces the conversation and so ends the turn that asked. An MCP client's loads fire nothing. The host sends a picture of the active plate with it and, when no purpose is recorded, one sentence of its own built from that state. The second is `slice-failed` (trigger `slice_failed`): a plate's last slice did not succeed, because OrcaSlicer's check refused the plate or the run failed. The workspace records why on the plate (`WorkspacePlate::slice_failure`: OrcaSlicer's words, the setting and object when named, refused or failed) at the moment it happens, from the print-issue monitor's reading, and keeps it until the plate slices. The skill fires on the first request that carries a failure the model has not read about: the result of the model's own `slice_start`, the turn the app starts when a slice the model started fails after its turn, or the next request after the person's Slice all. Each failure fires once; the same refusal again is the same failure.
